---
title: "MCP Events"
description: "Experimental. Let an MCP server offer your events to agents as MCP Events subscriptions."
---

{% callout type="warning" %}
Experimental. The MCP Events spec ([SEP-3415](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3415)) is still a draft, so this feature and its behavior may change without notice. It is off by default.
{% /callout %}

MCP Events lets an agent subscribe to your events through an MCP server. You run the MCP server. It authenticates its users and passes each `events/subscribe` and `events/unsubscribe` to Outpost, which verifies the callback URL, stores the subscription and delivers matching events to it, signed with [Standard Webhooks](https://www.standardwebhooks.com/). The MCP Events spec defines the challenge, errors and rate limits; this page covers what you set up in Outpost.

## Enable MCP Events

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}

Turn on MCP Events and define your events in [Hookdeck MCP Events settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/mcp-events).

{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}

Set `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_ENABLED` to `true` and define the events with `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_EVENTS_SCHEMA`. Subscription lifetimes, callback verification and insecure callbacks for development have their own options. See [MCP Events configuration](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental).

{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

## Define events

Event definitions list the events your MCP server offers and the arguments an agent can filter each one by. They use the format of an MCP `events/list` result:

```json
{
  "events": [
    {
      "name": "order.created",
      "description": "A new order was placed.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "status": { "type": "string", "enum": ["paid", "pending"] }
        }
      },
      "payloadSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "order_id": { "type": "string" },
          "status": { "type": "string" },
          "total": { "type": "number" }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

An agent subscribing to `order.created` can pass `{"status": "paid"}` and receives only the orders whose `status` is `paid`.

- `name` is a topic, unique in the list. With `TOPICS` set, it must be one of them. Wildcards aren't allowed.
- `payloadSchema` is a required JSON Schema object that describes the event's `data`.
- `inputSchema` is an optional JSON Schema object that lists the arguments under `properties`. Without it, every top-level `payloadSchema` property that is a string, number, integer or boolean is an optional argument.
- Each argument is a string, number, integer or boolean, and names a top-level field of the payload with a compatible type. Outpost delivers an event only if every argument equals the field of the same name.
- `description`, `delivery` and `_meta` are optional. When `delivery` is set it must include `webhook`.

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}

Import the JSON (or YAML) in [Hookdeck MCP Events settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/mcp-events), or edit the events there.

{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}

Set the JSON in [`EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_EVENTS_SCHEMA`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental), inline or as the path of a JSON or YAML file.

{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

Invalid definitions are reported with the event and the field. Outpost still starts, and MCP Events answers `503` until they are fixed. Without definitions, every subscription is refused.

`GET /mcp/events/list` (Admin API key) returns the configured events as an MCP `events/list` result, for your MCP server to serve as `events/list`. `topics=a,b` restricts it to those events, and `limit` and `cursor` page through it.

## Connect your MCP server

Your MCP server calls Outpost with the Admin API key, on behalf of a user it has authenticated:

- `POST /tenants/<TENANT_ID>/mcp/events/subscribe`
- `POST /tenants/<TENANT_ID>/mcp/events/unsubscribe`

The body is `{"principal": ..., "params": ...}`: `principal` identifies the user, and `params` are the MCP request's params as received. Outpost trusts the `principal` and creates the tenant if it is missing. Checking that the user may receive an event with those arguments is up to your MCP server, as the spec requires.

```sh
curl -X POST '{% $OUTPOST_API_BASE_URL %}/tenants/<TENANT_ID>/mcp/events/subscribe' \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>' \
--header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
--data '{
  "principal": "user_42",
  "params": {
    "name": "order.created",
    "arguments": { "status": "paid" },
    "delivery": {
      "mode": "webhook",
      "url": "https://agent.example.com/hooks/orders",
      "secret": "whsec_C2FVsBQIhrscChlQIMV+b5sSYspob7oD"
    },
    "ttlMs": 3600000
  }
}'
```

```json
{
  "resultType": "complete",
  "id": "sub_9f2c1e",
  "refreshBefore": "2026-11-11T13:00:00Z",
  "cursor": null,
  "truncated": false
}
```

On success, the answer is the MCP result to return to the agent. On failure, it is a `422` with an `mcp_error` (`code`, `message`, and `data` where the spec defines it) for your MCP server to return as the JSON-RPC error. See the [API reference](/docs/outpost/api) for the full request and responses.

Subscribing again with the same principal, event, arguments and callback URL refreshes the subscription: the same `id`, a new expiry and the new secret, which signs alongside the old one for 24 hours. The lifetime follows `ttlMs`, within the limits set in [configuration](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental). Once it has passed, the subscription receives no events.

Unsubscribing takes the same `principal` and the `params` of the original subscribe, and answers `{"resultType": "complete"}`.

## How subscriptions differ from destinations

- The destinations API never returns them. They are not listed, and can't be created, read, updated or deleted there; only the subscribe endpoints manage them.
- They don't count toward `MAX_DESTINATIONS_PER_TENANT` or a tenant's `destinations_count`. Their events are part of the tenant's `topics`, and changes to them emit `tenant.subscription.updated`.
- Deliveries are retried automatically: up to 5 attempts within 15 minutes. `RETRY_SCHEDULE` and `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` don't apply, and a manual retry of a subscription's attempt answers `404`. A `410` or `413` response is not retried.
- A subscription is bound to its principal: only that principal can refresh or remove it.
- Attempts and events keep the subscription's `id` as `destination_id`. Publishing to a subscription by `destination_id` matches nothing.
- Each delivery carries `X-MCP-Subscription-Id`. The envelope's `cursor` is always `null`: there is no replay.
